      "id": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/26/meta-stock-offering-ai.html",
      "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/26/meta-stock-offering-ai.html",
      "title": "Meta launches $95 billion stock raise to finance AI infrastructure",
      "content_html": "Meta Platforms, the parent company of Facebook, Instagram and WhatsApp, said on Oct. 26 it plans to raise up to $95 billion through stock sales to finance artificial-intelligence infrastructure and develop what it calls personal superintelligence. The plan begins with about $35 billion in public offerings—$22.5 billion of Class A common stock and $12.5 billion of mandatory convertible preferred shares—followed by a separate at-the-market sale of up to $60 billion in new, non-voting Class C shares.\n\nMeta reported negative free cash flow of $2.12 billion in the third quarter, its first negative free cash flow in more than a decade, and raised its 2026 capital-expenditure forecast to $135 billion-$145 billion. The company says it will also use operating cash flow, investment-grade debt and partnerships to fund its spending. Its latest quarterly filing showed $82.45 billion in cash and marketable securities and $80.92 billion in long-term debt as of Sept. 30.\n\nMeta expects the Class C sale to begin after those shares start trading in the first quarter of 2027; the company has not finalized the timing or terms of that $60 billion program. Nvidia, Advanced Micro Devices and Broadcom have expressed interest in buying up to $5 billion of the initial Class A offering.",
